        <tr id="comment-body-16556008"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p> <a href="https://jira-sd.mc1.oracleiaas.com/browse/SDR-31649" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">https://jira-sd.mc1.oracleiaas.com/browse/SDR-31649</a> has been approved. <a href="https://jira.oci.oraclecorp.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=alexamun" class="user-hover" rel="alexamun">Alexandru Munteanu</a> can you close this ticket?</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-16511924"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                        <a class="user-hover" rel="etyuan" id="word_commented_etyuan" href="https://jira.oci.oraclecorp.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=etyuan">Ethan Yuan</a>
                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">07/Jul/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-16511924"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Created an SDR ticket - <a href="https://jira-sd.mc1.oracleiaas.com/browse/SDR-31649" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">https://jira-sd.mc1.oracleiaas.com/browse/SDR-31649</a> </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-16468788"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                        <a class="user-hover" rel="alexamun" id="word_commented_alexamun" href="https://jira.oci.oraclecorp.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=alexamun">Alexandru Munteanu</a>
                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">01/Jul/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-16468788"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>We discussed on a dedicated meeting:</p>
<ul>
	<li>Today, the admin task is actually more secure from an employee perspective =&gt; Since while the API itself doesn't have any authentication, getting to a point where the engineer can execute the admin task requires multiple levels of access =&gt; Needs cluster access permission  + hostclass entitlement permission  (which today we handle through a dedicated role <a href="https://permissions.oci.oraclecorp.com/service/odbgcp?tab=prod-cluster-login" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">https://permissions.oci.oraclecorp.com/service/odbgcp?tab=prod-cluster-login</a> ) ; Also executing the admin task requires following up a dedicated runbook</li>
	<li>The concern with moving admin tasks to APIs is setting up the proper authorization model. Most teams in OCI use blanket boat groups that grant "manage all-resources") access, and that is harder to contain than it sounds.. ALso adds the risk of new employees adding a blanket policy that grants too much access to the new APIs. Especially since these APIs will have dedicated permission strings, security central can't auto-detect that a non-JIT group might have access to dangerous APIs</li>
	<li>Long term we will try to move to away from admin tasks, but I think that can be more dangerous unless done right; Other teams that are asked directly to move away, may unknowingly make it less secure unless they understand the implications of virtual resourcing model</li>
	<li>Currently, our Admin tasks are all about checking or updating very specific fields in kiev. They are ONLY used in rare events to fix the state of a specific resource. 
	<ul>
		<li>Gaining access to these APIs by a malicious user can affect ControlPlane operations with the resources, but cannot gain access to the resources themselves (dataplane). </li>
		<li>Using Kiev transactions we could potentially revert any wrong-doing should an attack happen</li>
		<li>In worst case, customers will just stop seeing their resources in the Cloud Provider console, but will keep seeing them in OCI console</li>
	</ul>
	</li>
	<li>Access to these tasks can only happen inside our VCN; there is NO direct access available to these admin tasks from outside the network. Hence an attacker would have first to run their code inside the VCN, in order to even access the admin tasks.</li>
	<li>Given all of the above, I do think the risk for the current admin tasks is minimal from an attacker perspective, and it's potential riskier to move away from them from an employee access perspective, without proper guidelines.</li>
</ul>


<p> </p>
        </td></tr>
